   "en": {
    "headline": "A task queue in git against the exhaustion of managing many coding agents at once",
    "summary_short": "Jumping between twenty Claude Code or Codex sessions drains attention. A developer proposes treating the work as a queue and closing each task before moving to the next.",
    "summary_long": "The problem is familiar to anyone coding with agents: while one replies, another task gets opened, and soon there are twenty threads to keep in mind. The author calls it \"brain fry\" and thinks it fails on two fronts: task sequencing and context. Instead of advice like taking breaks or limiting scope, the author proposes a system of their own.\n\nEach task is a markdown file in the repository, next to the code. An agent works that board in cycles: it researches, builds what has been approved, runs the tests and never deploys to production. Anything that needs a human decision lands in a prioritised inbox. The author takes one item, decides and puts it back in the queue, so it doesn't stay pending in their head. It is still exhausting, they admit, but they no longer get lost.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": []
   },
